window.__finlexDocLoaded(10072,{"id":10072,"articles":[{"id":1000226,"no":"Overview","heading":"Official Overview & Scope (IFRS Foundation)","text":"IFRS 15 is effective for annual reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2018, with earlier application permitted.\n\nIFRS 15 establishes the principles that an entity applies when reporting information about the nature, amount, timing and uncertainty of revenue and cash flows from a contract with a customer. Applying IFRS 15, an entity recognises revenue to depict the transfer of promised goods or services to the customer in an amount that reflects the consideration to which the entity expects to be entitled in exchange for those goods or services.\n\nTo recognise revenue under IFRS 15, an entity applies the following five steps:\n\nidentify the contract(s) with a customer.\n\nidentify the performance obligations in the contract. Performance obligations are promises in a contract to transfer to a customer goods or services that are distinct.\n\ndetermine the transaction price. The transaction price is the amount of consideration to which an entity expects to be entitled in exchange for transferring promised goods or services to a customer. If the consideration promised in a contract includes a variable amount, an entity must estimate the amount of consideration to which it expects to be entitled in exchange for transferring the promised goods or services to a customer.\n\nallocate the transaction price to each performance obligation on the basis of the relative stand-alone selling prices of each distinct good or service promised in the contract.\n\nrecognise revenue when a performance obligation is satisfied by transferring a promised good or service to a customer (which is when the customer obtains control of that good or service). A performance obligation may be satisfied at a point in time (typically for promises to transfer goods to a customer) or over time (typically for promises to transfer services to a customer). For a performance obligation satisfied over time, an entity would select an appropriate measure of progress to determine how much revenue should be recognised as the performance obligation is satisfied.","seq":0},{"id":1000227,"no":"IFRS 15.1–8","heading":"Objective & Scope","text":"Core principle (IFRS 15.2): recognise revenue to depict the transfer of promised goods or services to customers in an amount reflecting the consideration to which the entity expects to be entitled.\n\n[Scope Exclusions (IFRS 15.5–8)]\n• Lease contracts within IFRS 16 Leases\n• Insurance contracts within IFRS 17 Insurance Contracts\n• Financial instruments and other contractual rights/obligations within IFRS 9, IFRS 10, IFRS 11, IAS 27, and IAS 28\n• Non-monetary exchanges between entities in the same line of business to facilitate sales to customers\n• Partially in-scope contracts (IFRS 15.7): apply separation/measurement rules of other standards first, then apply IFRS 15 to the remainder.","seq":1},{"id":1000228,"no":"IFRS 15.9–21","heading":"Step 1: Identifying the Contract & Contract Modifications","text":"[Five Cumulative Contract Criteria (IFRS 15.9)]\n• Parties have approved the contract and are committed to perform their respective obligations\n• Each party's rights regarding goods or services to be transferred can be identified\n• Payment terms for the goods or services can be identified\n• Contract has commercial substance (risk, timing, or amount of future cash flows expected to change)\n• Collection of consideration is probable (evaluated considering customer's ability and intention to pay when due)\n\n[Combination & Modifications (IFRS 15.17–21)]\n• Combine two or more contracts entered into at or near the same time with the same customer if negotiated as a package with a single commercial objective, consideration depends on another contract, or goods/services form a single performance obligation (IFRS 15.17).\n• Account for a contract modification as a separate contract when it adds distinct goods/services at their stand-alone selling prices (IFRS 15.20).\n• Otherwise account prospectively (termination of old contract + creation of new contract) if remaining goods/services are distinct, or via cumulative catch-up adjustment if remaining goods/services are not distinct (IFRS 15.21).","seq":2},{"id":1000229,"no":"IFRS 15.22–30","heading":"Step 2: Identifying Performance Obligations","text":"At contract inception, assess promised goods or services and identify each distinct good or service (or a series of distinct goods/services that are substantially the same with the same pattern of transfer, IFRS 15.22–23).\n\n[Two-Step Distinctness Test (IFRS 15.27–29)]\n• Capable of being distinct (IFRS 15.27(a)): the customer can benefit from the good or service either on its own or together with other readily available resources.\n• Distinct within the context of the contract (IFRS 15.27(b) & 15.29): the promise to transfer the good or service is separately identifiable from other promises—indicators of NOT separately identifiable include significant integration service, significant modification/customisation, or high interdependence/interrelation.","seq":3},{"id":1000230,"no":"IFRS 15.46–72","heading":"Step 3: Determining the Transaction Price","text":"Transaction price is the amount of consideration an entity expects to be entitled to in exchange for transferring promised goods or services, excluding amounts collected on behalf of third parties such as sales taxes/VAT (IFRS 15.47).\n\n[Key Measurement Components]\n• Variable consideration (IFRS 15.50–59): estimate discounts, rebates, refunds, credits, performance bonuses, or penalties using expected value or most likely amount; include only to the extent it is highly probable that a significant reversal in cumulative revenue will not occur (the variable consideration constraint, IFRS 15.56).\n• Significant financing component (IFRS 15.60–65): adjust consideration for the time value of money when timing provides a significant financing benefit; practical expedient allows ignoring financing if the gap between transfer and payment is one year or less (IFRS 15.63).\n• Non-cash consideration (IFRS 15.66–69): measure at fair value (or indirectly by reference to stand-alone selling price of promised goods/services).\n• Consideration payable to a customer (IFRS 15.70–72): reduce transaction price unless the payment is for a distinct good or service received from the customer.","seq":4},{"id":1000231,"no":"IFRS 15.73–90","heading":"Step 4: Allocating the Transaction Price","text":"Allocate the transaction price to each performance obligation in proportion to its relative stand-alone selling price (SSP) determined at contract inception (IFRS 15.73–76).\n\n[Estimating Stand-Alone Selling Price (IFRS 15.78–79)]\n• Observable price when sold separately in similar circumstances is the best evidence (IFRS 15.77).\n• If not directly observable, estimate using: Adjusted market assessment approach, Expected cost plus a margin approach, or Residual approach (permitted only when SSP is highly variable or uncertain, IFRS 15.79(c)).\n• Discounts (IFRS 15.81–83) and variable consideration (IFRS 15.84–86) are allocated proportionately to all performance obligations unless specific criteria show they relate entirely to one or more specific obligations.","seq":5},{"id":1000232,"no":"IFRS 15.31–45","heading":"Step 5: Satisfying Performance Obligations (Over Time vs. Point in Time)","text":"Recognise revenue when (or as) the entity satisfies a performance obligation by transferring control of a promised good or service to the customer (IFRS 15.31–33).\n\n[Over-Time Recognition — Any ONE of Three Criteria (IFRS 15.35)]\n• (a) Customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefits as the entity performs (e.g., routine cleaning/security services);\n• (b) Entity's performance creates or enhances an asset that the customer controls as it is created or enhanced (e.g., building on customer's land);\n• (c) Entity's performance does not create an asset with an alternative use to the entity AND the entity has an enforceable right to payment for performance completed to date (IFRS 15.35(c), 15.36–37).\n• Progress measurement (IFRS 15.39–45): select a single output or input method per obligation that faithfully depicts performance; exclude uninstalled materials or wasted inputs from cost-to-cost progress.\n\n[Point-in-Time Control Transfer Indicators (IFRS 15.38)]\n• Entity has a present right to payment; customer has legal title; entity has transferred physical possession; customer has significant risks and rewards of ownership; customer has accepted the asset.","seq":6},{"id":1000233,"no":"IFRS 15.91–104","heading":"Contract Costs (Acquisition & Fulfilment)","text":"• Incremental costs of obtaining a contract (IFRS 15.91–94): capitalise costs that would not have been incurred if the contract had not been obtained (e.g., sales commissions) if expected to be recovered. Practical expedient: expense immediately if the amortisation period is one year or less (IFRS 15.94).\n• Costs to fulfil a contract (IFRS 15.95–98): if not within IAS 2, IAS 16, or IAS 38, capitalise only when costs relate directly to a contract, generate or enhance resources used in satisfying future obligations, and are expected to be recovered. General/administrative costs and wasted materials are expensed as incurred.\n• Amortisation & impairment (IFRS 15.99–104): amortise on a systematic basis consistent with the transfer of related goods/services; recognise impairment if carrying amount exceeds remaining expected consideration minus directly related remaining costs.","seq":7},{"id":1000234,"no":"IFRS 15.105–129","heading":"Presentation & Disclosures","text":"• Statement of financial position presentation (IFRS 15.105–109): present a contract liability when payment is received (or unconditional right to payment arises) before transfer; present a contract asset when performance precedes unconditional right to consideration; present a receivable (under IFRS 9) when the right to consideration is unconditional (only passage of time required).\n• Core disclosures (IFRS 15.110–129): disaggregation of revenue by economic factors; contract balances and revenue recognised from opening contract liabilities; remaining performance obligations (transaction price allocated and expected timing of recognition, IFRS 15.120); significant judgements on timing, transaction price, and SSP; capitalised contract costs.","seq":8},{"id":1000235,"no":"IFRS 15.B1–B89","heading":"Key Application Guidance (Appendix B Hot Topics)","text":"• Principal vs. Agent (IFRS 15.B34–B38): an entity is a principal (gross revenue) if it controls the specified good/service before transfer to the customer; control indicators include primary responsibility for fulfilment, inventory risk, and discretion in establishing prices (B37). Otherwise it is an agent (net fee/commission).\n• Sale with a right of return (IFRS 15.B20–B27): recognise revenue for expected retained goods, a refund liability for expected returns, and a right-of-return asset (adjusted for recovery costs).\n• Warranties (IFRS 15.B28–B33): assurance-type warranties are accounted for under IAS 37; service-type warranties (customer has option to purchase separately or warranty provides an additional service) are a separate performance obligation.\n• Customer options for additional goods/services (IFRS 15.B39–B43): if the option provides a material right (e.g., loyalty points, volume renewal discounts), allocate transaction price to the material right.\n• Breakage / unexercised rights (IFRS 15.B44–B47): recognise expected breakage proportionately as customers exercise rights (if entitled), otherwise when likelihood of exercise becomes remote.\n• Licensing of intellectual property (IFRS 15.B52–B63B): right to access IP (over time) vs. right to use IP (point in time); sales- or usage-based royalties exception (B63) recognises revenue only when the subsequent sale/usage occurs.\n• Repurchase agreements (IFRS 15.B64–B76): forwards and call options are accounted for as a lease (IFRS 16) or financing arrangement, not a sale; put options depend on whether customer has a significant economic incentive to exercise.\n• Consignment & bill-and-hold (IFRS 15.B77–B82): bill-and-hold requires substantive reason, separate identification, readiness for physical transfer, and inability of seller to redirect the product.","seq":9},{"id":1000236,"no":"CAS 14 vs IFRS 15","heading":"Cross-Framework Practical Comparison (China CAS 14 vs. IFRS 15)","text":"China CAS 14 (2017 Revision) is substantially converged with IFRS 15 in its five-step model, over-time vs. point-in-time criteria, principal vs. agent assessment, and contract cost rules.\n\n[Key Practical Nuances to Watch]\n• Terminology & mapping: IFRS 15.35 corresponds directly to CAS 14 Art. 11; IFRS 15.B34–B38 (Principal vs. Agent) corresponds to CAS 14 Art. 34 and MOF Revenue Implementation Q&As.\n• Assurance-type warranty classification: under CAS (MOF Interpretation No. 18, 2024), assurance-type warranty expense is presented in Cost of Sales rather than Selling Expenses; under IFRS 18/IAS 1 it depends on nature/function classification.\n• Disclosure granularity: IFRS 15.110–129 mandates quantitative disclosure of remaining performance obligations (backlog) and detailed contract balance roll-forwards, which listed entities reporting under IFRS must maintain beyond basic CAS statutory notes.","seq":10},{"id":1000237,"no":"History 1","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In April 2001 the International Accounting Standards Board (Board) adopted IAS 11 Construction Contracts and IAS 18 Revenue, both of which had originally been issued by the International Accounting Standards Committee (IASC) in December 1993. IAS 18 replaced a previous version: Revenue Recognition (issued in December 1982). IAS 11 replaced parts of IAS 11 Accounting for Construction Contracts (issued in March 1979).\n\nIn December 2001 the Board issued SIC‑31 Revenue—Barter Transactions Involving Advertising Services. The Interpretation was originally developed by the Standards Interpretations Committee of the IASC to determine the circumstances in which a seller of advertising services can reliably measure revenue at the fair value of advertising services provided in a barter transaction.\n\nIn June 2007 the Board issued IFRIC 13 Customer Loyalty Programmes. The Interpretation was developed by the IFRS Interpretations Committee (the ‘Interpretations Committee’) to address the accounting by the entity that grants award credits to its customers.","seq":11},{"id":1000238,"no":"History 2","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In July 2008 the Board issued IFRIC 15 Agreements for the Construction of Real Estate. The Interpretation was developed by the Interpretations Committee to apply to the accounting for revenue and associated expenses by entities that undertake the construction of real estate directly or through subcontractors.\n\nIn January 2009 the Board issued IFRIC 18 Transfers of Assets from Customers. The Interpretation was developed by the Interpretations Committee to apply to the accounting for transfers of items of property, plant and equipment by entities that receive such transfers from their customers.\n\nIn May 2014 the Board issued IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers, together with the introduction of Topic 606 into the Financial Accounting Standards Board’s Accounting Standards Codification®. IFRS 15 replaces IAS 11, IAS 18, IFRIC 13, IFRIC 15, IFRIC 18 and SIC‑31. IFRS 15 provides a comprehensive framework for recognising revenue from contracts with customers.","seq":12},{"id":1000239,"no":"History 3","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In September 2015 the Board issued Effective Date of IFRS 15 which deferred the mandatory effective date of IFRS 15 to 1 January 2018.\n\nIn April 2016 the Board issued Clarifications to IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers clarifying the Board’s intentions when developing some of the requirements in IFRS 15. These amendments do not change the underlying principles of IFRS 15 but clarify how those principles should be applied and provide additional transitional relief.\n\nIn May 2017, the Board issued IFRS 17 Insurance Contracts which permits an entity to choose whether to apply IFRS 17 or IFRS 15 to specified fixed-fee service contracts that meet the definition of an insurance contract.","seq":13},{"id":1000240,"no":"History 4","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"Other Standards have made minor consequential amendments to IFRS 15, including IFRS 16 Leases (issued January 2016), Amendments to References to the Conceptual Framework in IFRS Standards (issued March 2018) and IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements (issued April 2024).","seq":14},{"id":1000241,"no":"Support","heading":"Related Projects & Implementation Support","text":"[Related completed projects]\n• Assessment of Promised Goods or Services (IFRS 15)\n• Clarifications to IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers\n• Compensation for Delays or Cancellations (IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers)\n• Costs to Fulfil a Contract (IFRS 15)\n• Over Time Transfer of Constructed Good (IAS 23)\n• Post-implementation Review of IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers\n• Principal versus Agent: Software Reseller (IFRS 15)\n• Rate-regulated Activities\n• Recognition of Revenue from Tuition Fees (IFRS 15)\n• Revenue Recognition in a Real Estate Contract (IFRS 15)\n• Revenue from Contracts with Customers\n• Revenue recognition in a real estate contract that includes the transfer of land (IFRS 15)\n• Right to payment for performance completed to date (IFRS 15)\n• Training Costs to Fulfil a Contract (IFRS 15)\n\n[Implementation support]\n• IFRS 15 supporting material","seq":15},{"id":1000242,"no":"References","heading":"Official Text Link & Public Practical Guidance","text":"Official IFRS Foundation Page (English): https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ifrs-15-revenue-from-contracts-with-customers/\nAccess Note: Free access to the unaccompanied text of IFRS Accounting Standards on ifrs.org requires a free registered IFRS.org account; Basis for Conclusions and Illustrative Examples require an IFRS Digital Subscription.\nPublic Companion Overview: KPMG Insights into IFRS — An Overview (Sept 2026): https://assets.kpmg.com/content/dam/kpmgsites/xx/pdf/ifrg/2026/isg-2026-insights-an-overview.pdf\nKPMG IFRS Accounting Toolkit (Standard Handbooks): https://kpmg.com/xx/en/what-we-do/services/audit/corporate-reporting-institute/ifrs/toolkit.html","seq":16}]});
